{"type": "mcq", "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>, the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"52 degree\"><mn>52</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"upper A upper C equals 30\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></math>. In triangle <math alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math>, the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"52 degree\"><mn>52</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"upper P upper R equals 120\"><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn>\n</math>. Which additional piece of information is sufficient to prove that triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is similar to triangle <math alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math>?</p>", "keys": ["8b2a0ead-37e0-4025-974b-4196522e9a72"], "answerOptions": [{"id": "ee3e50eb-b072-49a4-b058-5e261884b6e5", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper B equals 50\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></math> and <math alttext=\"upper P upper Q equals 50\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></math>.</p>"}, {"id": "b7cae251-b55c-469c-87df-ff888f7eb411", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"upper A upper B equals 50\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></math> and <math alttext=\"upper Q upper R equals 200\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn>\n</math>.</p>"}, {"id": "8b2a0ead-37e0-4025-974b-4196522e9a72", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measures of angle <math alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> and angle <math alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math> are <math alttext=\"32 degree\"><mn>32</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"96 degree\"><mn>96</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math>, respectively.</p>"}, {"id": "95ac1461-7f53-4502-83cc-7b881719a279", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measures of angle <math alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> and angle <math alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi></math> are <math alttext=\"52 degree\"><mn>52</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"32 degree\"><mn>32</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math>, respectively.</p>"}], "rationale": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"52 degree\"><mn>52</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"52 degree\"><mn>52</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Therefore, angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math> is congruent to angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P\"><mi>P</mi></math>. Choice C&nbsp;states that the measures of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> and angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"32 degree\"><mn>32</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"96 degree\"><mn>96</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, respectively. The triangle sum theorem states that the measures of the angles in a triangle sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. In triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>, if the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"32 degree\"><mn>32</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, it follows that the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 180 minus left parenthesis 52 plus 32 right parenthesis right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"96 degree\"><mn>96</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. In triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math>, if the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"96 degree\"><mn>96</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, it follows that the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 180 minus left parenthesis 52 plus 96 right parenthesis right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>96</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"32 degree\"><mn>32</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Therefore, in triangles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math>, angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> is congruent to angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi></math>, and angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math> is congruent to angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math>. If two triangles have three pairs of congruent angles, then the triangles are similar. Therefore, the additional piece of information that is sufficient to prove that triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is similar to triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is that the measures of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> and angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"32 degree\"><mn>32</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"96 degree\"><mn>96</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, respectively.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect&nbsp;and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect&nbsp;and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.&nbsp;</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. If the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"52 degree\"><mn>52</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, it follows that the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 180 minus left parenthesis 52 plus 52 right parenthesis right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"76 degree\"><mn>76</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. If the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Q\"><mi>Q</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"32 degree\"><mn>32</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, which isn&rsquo;t equal to any angle measure in triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>, the triangles can&rsquo;t be similar.</p>", "externalid": "669ffcd9-3468-483d-9398-56885814c9b7", "correct_answer": ["C"]}